{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Hypothesis-driven pass 1 (worktree deer-flake, git 2.55 local; CI failure was git 2.54/ubuntu, pytest 9.1.1 + xdist 3.8.0).\n\nMECHANISM FINGERPRINT (proven by direct injection): building the exact fixture history and unlinking .git/objects/66/842fd0... between the 1005 commit and the merge reproduces the CI signature EXACTLY — rc 128, 'error: invalid object 100644 66842fd0... for aaaa-bbbb-cccc-dddd/1005-cccc2222-COMMENT.json' printed TWICE, 'fatal: git write-tree failed to write a tree'. Also verified: json.dumps of the fixture's 1005 payload hashes to 66842fd0... — git add hashed the CORRECT bytes; the object then went missing.\n\nDiscriminators proven:\n- A zero-length/corrupt loose object does NOT reproduce it: merge exits 0 (the write-tree check is stat-only). The CI failure therefore requires ENOENT — the loose file (or its fanout dir) was ABSENT at merge, milliseconds after 'git commit' of the same blob succeeded (commit's own write_index_as_tree verifies the blob too, so it was present at commit time).\n- gc --auto / gc / prune / repack -adq run between commit and merge do NOT reproduce it: the blob is reachable (packed, still findable), merge succeeds. gc cannot prune a fresh reachable object; auto-gc threshold (~6700 loose) is unreachable in a ~30-object repo. Hypothesis (b) as *normal, completed* maintenance: KILLED. A background maintenance process remains conceivable only as a crashed/interrupted repack, but nothing in this repo triggers maintenance at all pre-merge.\n\n(a) GIT_* env leakage: KILLED. grep of the whole suite + src: no os.environ direct assignment of GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY/GIT_DIR/GIT_INDEX_FILE/GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES/GIT_COMMON_DIR anywhere; all GIT_* settings are monkeypatch.setenv (function-scoped, main-thread reverted) of harmless keys (GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL/SYSTEM, GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH, GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES). Also logically inconsistent: a leaked object-dir during add+commit of 1005 would strand the COMMIT+TREE objects in the alternate store too, and the merge would then fail resolving HEAD, not at write-tree with only the blob missing.\n(c) global git config: KILLED. CI writes only identity + init.defaultBranch (all workflows grepped); nothing destructive; no test writes --global (the two --global reads are env-isolated).\n(d) tmp_path 30-char truncation collision: KILLED. Suite-wide scan of all test defs truncated to 30 chars: neither test_a_git_failure_falls_back_ nor test_ledger_attribution_is_unc has a duplicate; failing dirs were _0 (first use). Workers own disjoint popen-gwN basetemps; both CI steps use distinct basetemps and run sequentially.\n\nSelection-bias note: this fixture's merge (trivial in-index merge -> write-tree) is one of the only operations in the 13k-test suite that VERIFIES loose-object presence for a whole directory, so ANY rare loose-object disappearance anywhere in the suite would preferentially surface at exactly this line — consistent with two hits in this module (both ubuntu, both popen-gw1) and nowhere else.\n\nNext: bounded stress reproduction attempt, then (per plan) the instrumentation path if the deleter stays unidentified."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786691600660075001,"uuid":"f9c5634a-8ee6-4a52-b9cf-893b8da7a81c"}